WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.580
After authentication, the application needs a way to remember identity across requests.

2
00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:12.360
Traditional server-side sessions use a session identifier stored in a cookie.

3
00:00:12.460 --> 00:00:17.420
The server keeps the session state, while the browser sends the identifier with each request.

4
00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:24.000
That identifier is sensitive because anyone who can use it may be treated as the authenticated user.

5
00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:34.180
Secure session design uses unpredictable identifiers, rotation after important events, expiration, and careful invalidation on logout or account changes.

6
00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:37.340
Cookie settings help protect session identifiers.

7
00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:41.680
The Secure attribute limits transmission to HTTPS.

8
00:00:41.780 --> 00:00:45.780
HttpOnly helps prevent client-side scripts from reading the cookie.

9
00:00:45.880 --> 00:00:51.240
SameSite can reduce some cross-site request risks when configured appropriately for the application flow.

10
00:00:51.340 --> 00:00:54.380
Cookie protection does not replace authorization.

11
00:00:54.480 --> 00:01:02.080
It reduces the chance that the session token leaks or is misused, but every sensitive operation still needs a server-side permission decision.

12
00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:07.400
Token-based systems commonly use access tokens and refresh tokens.

13
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:13.980
Access tokens represent authorization to call an API or access a resource for a limited time.

14
00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:20.720
Refresh tokens may allow the client to obtain new access tokens over a longer period, which makes them highly sensitive.

15
00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:26.980
JWTs are a common token format, but a JWT is not safe just because it is signed.

16
00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:35.720
Developers must validate issuer, audience, expiration, signature, algorithm expectations, and relevant claims.

17
00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:43.580
Tokens should not be logged, placed in URLs, stored carelessly in browser storage, or sent to services that do not need them.
